on compute\",\"but isnt it here: KAGGLE_API_KEY in env?\",\"why cant u access: cell_tracking/.env????\\n\\nit is untracked naturally, and it contains other wandb, gurobi and runpod api keys..\",\"approved\",\"you have been taking 24h processing this entire request (excluding the brief pauses), what would u need done manually that can get us to the next stage much more quickly, or are you still evaluating models locally\",\"ok can you advise on the best model in terms of similar quality, much faster inference\",\"just to confirm, what is our current status- what models are promising, what are you evaluating, what is failing, and what blockers are there- what is the literature validating your chosen models? quote exact sentences/results from relevant papers\",\"do you think segmenting the 87 gb material into one 20-30GB validation/scoring set that is never included in the training can help us objectively determine the model strength and actively prevent overfitting\",\"can we not pre-train models so as to achieve much rapid convergence? we have wandb to store weights etc, so the goal here is to pre-train in a manner that allows the model to learn the zebrafish embryonic cell division/migration behaviour rapidly\",\"greatly reduce the threshold needed for goal closure, as this has been running for >72h. I want the goal to be realistic, and literature-validated, while showing the clearest path towards efficient pseudo-labelling-based SSL model training that ensures the most score-refinement relative to compute-time. \\n\\nmethods include:\\nDynamic Thresholding: Instead of running time-consuming static thresholds, algorithms dynamically adjust the threshold (often using Curriculum Learning) so that the model progressively uses more unlabeled data as it becomes more confident.Momentum Models & Consistency Regularization: Methods like Mean Teacher or FixMatch avoid multi-stage retraining bottlenecks by using a slowly-updating \\\"teacher\\\" model to generate pseudo-labels in real-time, while a \\\"student\\\" model is trained simultaneously on strongly augmented versions of the data\"],\"client-new-thread:9379a171-9dcd-4e49-968e-fbc23869494a\":[\"cruise config class stall indefinitely, and all configs shouldnt be permanent/globally enforced- users can just request for a higher or lower level default worker, request a change in default manner (creative/normal/professional) etc via natural language requests on codex ie $cruise: xxx. Also cruise worker --level n should work as a --level flag to automatically control model choice- this is the highest priority, overriding default behaviour, and only fires if the user explicitly requests a default model higher/lower than the current default\"],\"019f6efd-dbb3-7580-abc3-0d8562d4a19e\":[\"create additional slides that just compares the OTH, AD, DP, samples results \",\"besides just the vcf results\",\"and this should be comparing the newest sample results after debugging the command used as per the latest outputs\",\"let's go thru the content of the slides- and also what exactly cellsnp does- what are ad, oth, dp, sample lists, vcf - and explain what methods have been used to achieve the low-memory, cpu time speed up, estimated further runtime speedup when deployed on the RHEL cluster/increasing nprocs (how parallelisable are each of the modes). Also explain in detail the modes 1-3 from the original cellsnp documentation and their respective commands (in comparison with what was actually used in the comparisons between python and c++ runs - ie is the python run fully 100% aligned with the documented method- how that repo was designed to be used most optimally)\"],\"client-new-thread:afcbb3c9-cba7-46d2-ae46-15654adf5f02\":[\"/goal [$cruise:goal](<USER_HOME>/.codex/plugins/cache/agent-local/cruise/0.2.1+codex.20260717/skills/goal/SKILL.md) currently cleaning_notifier is not working despite its webhook being added to line. [$line](<USER_HOME>/.codex/skills/line/SKILL.md) \\n\\nwhen i text it it gives no response. \\nto properly test the notifier app, it is supposed to respond to me asking for a code for my user to initiate the cron notification mechanism where it should be texting me the simulated assignment for the next day at 8pm daily and then follow up if i do not select an option/reply by 10am the next day (on the assignment day). etc , follow the existing context- it should also follow up at 2:30pm on the same day if i do not already tell it i have completed cleaning the assigned rooms (no issues), and ensuring the issue user path works, and also automatically notify the manager (which should then trigger the consolidated hook and notify me also) if the cleaning company (me, the test user) did not respond in time about the assignment or about completing the assignment or if there are issues with the assignment)\"],\"019f73f9-64af-7f80-9803-78a804139ae6\":[\"I've added it and sent a msg but it only replies with the generic:\\n\\nThanks for your message!\\n\\nUnfortunately, this account isn't set up to reply directly to messages.\\nStay tuned for our next message (moon smile)\\n\\nalso, there should be at least 2 buttons for all cases. so not just approve but approve/request change. Request change will ask the manager what they would need to adjust (number of rooms to clean, or contractor unable to fulfill assignment altogether or non-plan related, but requesting change to other cleaning arrangements like a different cleaning schedule, cleaning method, misc proposals, for non-plan-related decide if it is more appropriate to just show a label over the text field for the user to type out their proposal, for plan-related which means reducing rooms cleaned/inability to fulfill, there can be option selectors for that.  (decide if this is too convoluted, the goal is simplciity)\\n\\nanyways also inherit all other functions from cleaner_bot while noting that cleaner_bot's target audience is cleaners/inspectors themselves while cleaning_notifier is intended to be a cronbot that fetches data from cloudbeds and deterministically sends the correct rooms/pins to be cleaned to the cleaning company (ie the audience is the group/point-of-contact manager)\"],\"client-new-thread:bb2f5478-5d25-4ac6-8df3-0670d65b8842\":[\"Based on the documents here- adhere closely in alignment with the recommendations (notably the positive examples are in important_notes and after page 24 of Olvl-SW&Essay.pdf), noting that important definitions and tips are distributed between pg16-24 of Olvl-SW&Essay.pdf and in hybrid essays.pdf, but of course lots of good context is available besides these), and draft out a well-grounded argumentative essay on the topic of \\\"Should students be allowed to use AI for schoolwork\\\" -- note this is based on SG, and our O-level English requirements \\n\\nNote, this topic is multifaceted, the assumptions are that students should only be allowed to use AI for schoolwork if it can be demonstrated that it benefits them educationally/ misc ways, teachers and parents both have roles to play in withholding/encouraging AI use in schoolwork, school policy/day-to-day assessments (of course for major termly tests/WA, all schools need to adhere to MOE's requirements) can change the incentives for students when it comes to schoolwork - how can intellectual curiosity and critical thinking be nurtured instead of getting spoonfed thoughts and opinions by AI from an early age - in essense the school has an outsized impact on how incentivised students are to use/not use AI in schoolwork... This is a zizo approach, but I encourage horizontal argumentation too, like exploring the full breadth of possible impact on the student etc\\n\\nI want you not to give me a straightforward essay, but rather the thought process behind each point, how you may elaborate and which examples (grounded by [$parallel-search](app://asdk_app_69fb9378663481919a68e8a2109644e5) ) you may give, this is for the purposes of teaching, and only after completing these steps that you look back and self-critique, before creating your final model essay (<600 words, must include this structure: Intro, P1/2/3 (one of the points shall have the structure: PEEL + Rebuttal + Counterargument with example if you state agree to a large extent, but if you agree to a limited extent, then you will concede to the rebuttal with no example, but remember to add the example to rebuttal in that case) -> regarding this, I want you to show both sides of the coin, when you do strongly agree, and limited disagree (so two model essays on 2 different stances, each with reasons/points/arguments/philosophical insights taken from existing corpus of reputable discourse on this issue - meaning from famous writers/academics/politicians/AI engineers/founders/lawyers/ etc high profile people)\"],\"019f761a-1434-7243-ba5b-d556e0fe2e40\":[\"of course, add a strong fitting conclusion to both essays too! (make it succinct though, intro too shd be succinct)\"],\"client-new-thread:69486f17-8d09-4c53-a23f-34a4b9dc82fb\":[\"Based on the documents here- adhere closely in alignment with the recommendations (notably the positive examples are in important_notes and after page 24 of Olvl-SW&Essay.pdf), noting that important definitions and tips are distributed between pg16-24 of Olvl-SW&Essay.pdf and in hybrid essays.pdf, but of course lots of good context is available besides these), and draft out a well-grounded argumentative essay on the topic of \\\"Should students be allowed to use AI for schoolwork\\\" -- note this is based on SG, and our O-level English requirements \\n\\nNote, this topic is multifaceted, the assumptions are that students should only be allowed to use AI for schoolwork if it can be demonstrated that it benefits them educationally/ misc ways, teachers and parents both have roles to play in withholding/encouraging AI use in schoolwork, school policy/day-to-day assessments (of course for major termly tests/WA, all schools need to adhere to MOE's requirements) can change the incentives for students when it comes to schoolwork - how can intellectual curiosity and critical thinking be nurtured instead of getting spoonfed thoughts and opinions by AI from an early age - in essense the school has an outsized impact on how incentivised students are to use/not use AI in schoolwork... This is a zizo approach, but I encourage horizontal argumentation too, like exploring the full breadth of possible impact on the student etc\\n\\nI want you not to give me a straightforward essay, but rather the thought process behind each point, how you may elaborate and which examples (grounded by [$parallel-search](app://asdk_app_69fb9378663481919a68e8a2109644e5) ) you may give, this is for the purposes of teaching, and only after completing these steps that you look back and self-critique, before creating your final model essay (<600 words, must include this structure: Intro, P1/2/3 (one of the points shall have the structure: PEEL + Rebuttal + Counterargument with example if you state agree to a large extent, but if you agree to a limited extent, then you will concede to the rebuttal with no example, but remember to add the example to rebuttal in that case) -> regarding this, I want you to show both sides of the coin, when you do strongly agree, and limited disagree (so two model essays on 2 different stances, each with reasons/points/arguments/philosophical insights taken from existing corpus of reputable discourse on this issue - meaning from famous writers/academics/politicians/AI engineers/founders/lawyers/ etc high profile people)\\n\\nof course make conclusion and intro both succinct with different approaches to intro/conclusion/peel (it can be sentence structure, different hook, different segues, different memorable closes etc)\\n\\nThis is for a Year 3 Olevel english student\"],\"client-new-thread:52913b4d-9699-4f1a-9de6-142515882c39\":[\"I want [$cruise:debug](<USER_HOME>/.codex/plugins/cache/agent-local/cruise/0.2.1+codex.20260717/skills/debug/SKILL.md) to be a similarly multi-functional tool like build, so the idea is to incorporate an inherent subagent-swarm capability to this skill - keep the maximum subagent worker level at level 3 or 4 (from Desktop/swarm?) that on use, if the user is requesting a general debug with no clear issue stated --> ci/quality control where lots of subagents will be given distinct user paths exploring the entire UX surface with aggressive attempts to break the software/website/tool etc \\n\\nfor an ambiguous request - ie stating the problem but not specifying a reproducible user path (a use case is if the user wants to automate bug ticket generation and his users are only complaining about buggy outcomes without telling how they got those bugs - the user path can be inferred from such an agent swarm that can narrow down a possible bug (once one bug is spotted, it can also immediately fix other related bugs if causes are similar - so identifying similar bugs with similar causes is helpful too)\\n\\nusers can config debug accordingly to reduce or increase its tendency to spawn subagent swarms/size of swarms (after all the codex container itself already restricts that, and this configs are only useful for pro users)\"],\"019f7642-b7d1-7fa3-85a9-298427509e81\":[\"/goal note if the user gave a comprehensive/sufficiently informative bug ticket: reproducibility path + bug outcome then it would not launch a subagent swarm at all, but it will be mapping by semantic similarity across the codebase to determine where the similar bug may appear elsewhere. This means that debug will have 2 modes, swarm and focussed modes\",\"Do not reinstall cruise at all\",\"continue\"],\"client-new-thread:1a263e1d-c9d3-46e3-afa2-b1f8b132df42\":[\"before implementing, let's map the codebase to determine feasibility, gate this at your implementation plan and await approval before proceeding \"],\"019f768a-764a-7292-87a2-c99c0f6ee625\":[\"note that this is tightly linked with consolid