ces- and given that my friend is able to use the api key for gemini generation, and similarly for me i can use it on cline vscode. This shows that your tests are flawed or you are not using the correct sdk to access gemini generation. \",\"rewrite the tests so they do not use codex's native env/my os's native env but rather uses my project folder's .env so that the checks are correctly showing that the gemini api key works\"],\"client-new-thread:c6168aae-a7af-4dc3-8fea-7b7c2149d2fd\":[\"no longer support vertex-ai and instead use my GEMIN_API_KEY\"],\"019f7aee-05c2-7053-866f-203a06434bfb\":[\"this uses the genai sdk, also i am no longer using vertex as im switching gcp accounts. Also note, the api key is in the local proj .env which isnt tracked! this repo is public so do not commit at all and always defer to manual commits, save this requirement to local proj memory/cruise + ensure all memory is made .gitignored\"],\"client-new-thread:a07846d7-7ebf-450c-a5db-6ec87fdbcaff\":[\"Based on this, determine if we should pause the currently-running goal:\\n\\n\\nThe metric is: `score = adjusted_edge_jaccard + 0.1 × division_jaccard`, hence the 1.1 ceiling. Let me check the actual run state versus the plan, since `GOAL.md` claims G-02 is inactive.\\n\\nCritical pattern: in every single local report, `score` exactly equals `adjusted_edge_jaccard` — meaning the division term has contributed **zero** in every run. Let me confirm the division situation and find the actual submissions.\\n\\nLet me confirm the submission history and check the competition timeline.\\n\\nZero scored submissions across 49 records. Now let me read the literature/research docs, which I need for your second question.\\n\\nThe research ledger is genuinely strong. Let me verify the external competition state — deadline and leaderboard structure.\\n\\nCompetition closes **September 22, 2026** — about two months out, $60k pool, 73 teams. Let me now assess what's actually built in the codebase, especially the division machinery.\\n\\nConfirmed: `enable_divisions: bool = False` by default, and the contract gates divisions behind edge-only CV ≥ 0.90 — a threshold never legitimately cleared. Let me check the leaderboard and the public baselines.\\n\\nKaggle's pages are JS-rendered so I can't scrape them; I'll work from your leaderboard numbers plus local evidence. Let me verify two technical levers in the official scorer.\\n\\nI have enough to give you a straight answer. Here's where things actually stand.\\n\\n## The bottom line\\n\\n**In four days of Codex goal-mode execution, zero Kaggle submissions have been scored.** Not one. I checked every record: 49 separate entries all carry `scored_submission: false`. The G-01 run before it ended with HTTP 403 on the submit call and that blocker was never resolved — it was reclassified as a stop condition rather than fixed.\\n\\nThe 0.903 number that the whole recent effort orbits around **is not yours**. `reports/g02/pubctl.json` records it plainly: it's the displayed score of a public notebook by another Kaggler (`yusuketogashi/biohub-another-approach`, script version 45), adopted as the \\\"accuracy parent.\\\" Your own general model, run over all 199 train videos, scores **0.658** (`fast_full_199_score`). The rule-based alpha's 12-clip audit is 0.8713. Everything between those numbers and 0.903 is someone else's pipeline.\\n\\nThe headline local number of 0.9026 is worse than it looks. Tracing it to `reports/g02/s97.json`, it came from a **two-frame clip with 3 ground-truth edges on one video and 9 on another**. `v6diag.json` records what happened when the same model was extended to eight frames: it collapsed to **0.764**, and the run was aborted with the note that \\\"a two-frame sparse-label clip is too small to establish lineage robustness.\\\"\\n\\n## The structural trap\\n\\nThis is the thing I'd most want you to see.\\n\\nThe scoring formula is `score = adjusted_edge_jaccard + 0.1 × division_jaccard`. Across every local report in the repo, `score` is *exactly equal* to `adjusted_edge_jaccard` — the division term has contributed zero, always. `division_tp: 0.0` appears 217 times; nonzero division TPs appear only in the synthetic scorer fixtures.\\n\\nThat's not an accident. `src/celltrack_alpha/types.py:94` sets `enable_divisions: bool = False`, and the G-02 contract gates divisions behind a hard rule: *\\\"Do not activate the division branch until edge-only grouped CV >= 0.90.\\\"* Edge-only CV has never legitimately reached 0.90 on any honest evaluation. So divisions stay locked, so the division term stays zero, so the ceiling stays at edge Jaccard alone — while divisions are precisely what separates the top of the leaderboard.\\n\\nThe gate was written to prevent premature optimization. In practice it has made the highest-value 0.1 of the metric permanently unreachable. Note the irony: the public V45 notebook you're benchmarking against *does* emit divisions (`safe_division_parent_um: 4.66`, `safe_division_sister_um: 8.5`), and its V103 sibling emits 429 of them. The artifact you'd actually submit has divisions; the pipeline you actually measure doesn't.\\n\\n## Why every experiment fails\\n\\nThe last two days produced eight rejection decisions and zero promotions. The most recent, four hours ago, rejected a candidate for a delta of **+0.00025** against a promotion threshold of +0.005.\\n\\nThe reason is a measurement-precision failure. Nearly every decision is made on a single held-out video (`44b6_24264f12`), often 1–8 frames, sometimes with **15 sparse ground-truth labels total** (`hyp2.json`). You cannot resolve a 0.005 effect with 15 labels — the standard error is an order of magnitude larger than the threshold. So the pipeline is guaranteed to reject nearly everything, including things that genuinely work, and the rejections carry no information. Four days of compute has been spent measuring noise against a threshold finer than the noise.\\n\\n## The literature, and how faithfully it's been applied\\n\\nThe research in `docs/alternative_tracking_approaches_research.md` and `doc_cell_tracking/RESEARCH.md` is genuinely good — better than most Kaggle write-ups. The mapping:\\n\\n**Rule-based DoG + Hungarian** rests on Jaqaman et al. 2008 (*Nat Methods*), the two-stage linear assignment formulation: frame-to-frame linking, then track-segment linking for gaps. The repo implements this correctly, including the augmented birth/death cost matrix and a proper separate gap-closing stage. This is the one part that's textbook-faithful.\\n\\n**The official baseline** (`TemporalUNet3D` + `SimpleNodeTransformer`) is the Royer Lab's sparse-supervised detector plus dense all-pairs association. The G-02 direction keeps the backbone and discards the dense transformer.\\n\\n**The sparse hybrid** is Linajea — Malin-Mayor et al. 2023 (*Nat Biotech*): sparse point supervision, learned center and backward-displacement outputs, global ILP over the candidate graph. This is the correct reference for this data, since the competition's sparse `(t,z,y,x)` GEFF annotations are almost exactly Linajea's supervision model. The repo borrowed the architecture ideas but never trained the model — the backward-displacement head (`Alpha 6`) is specified and never validated at scale.\\n\\n**Ultrack** (Bragantini et al. 2025, *Nat Methods*) is multi-hypothesis segmentation selected jointly with tracking under an ILP. `RESEARCH.md` correctly concludes it's a poor fit here — it needs genuine alternative segmentation hypotheses, and this pipeline produces sparse points, not masks. It was correctly deprioritized. Ultrack also never installed, because published releases need an older Python than the local 3.14.\\n\\n**Trackastra** (Gallusser & Weigert 2024) learns pairwise association over detections in a temporal window with explicit division handling. This is arguably the best-fit method in the entire review for the division problem specifically, and it was never tested.\\n\\nThe diagnosis is not that the literature was misread. It's that the review correctly identified the highest-value directions — learned association with division handling, backward-displacement parent prediction, node-count calibration — and then execution spent four days on compact-adapter fine-tuning experiments that the review itself ranked low.\\n\\n## Is there a path?\\n\\nYes, but not the current one. You have until **September 22, 2026** — roughly two months, 73 teams, $60k pool.\\n\\n**The single cheapest lever is one nobody has pulled.** The adjusted Jaccard is `J × (1 − 0.1 × (N_pred − N_true)/N_true)`, with `max(0, ·)` as the *only* bound — there is no upper cap. On the dense `6bba` video your pipeline predicts 2,550 nodes against 1,910 estimated: a ratio of 1.335, which multiplies your score by 0.8665. Raw edge Jaccard of 0.808 becomes 0.700. You are giving away 13% on dense videos to node over-prediction. And because there's no cap, *under*-predicting pays a bonus — the official fixture confirms an adjusted Jaccard of **1.07** at a node ratio of −0.7. High-precision, lower-recall detection is directly rewarded by this metric, and the repo has been tuning for recall.\\n\\nConcretely, in priority order:\\n\\n1. **Resolve the 403 and submit something.** You cannot be on a leaderboard you have never submitted to. This is the actual blocker and it's been open since G-01.\\n2. **Fix the evaluation before anything else.** Twenty videos minimum, not one; hundreds of labeled edges, not fifteen. Until then no experiment can inform any decision.\\n3. **Calibrate node count per scenario.** Cheapest real points on the board, worth an estimated +0.02–0.05 on dense videos, needs no training.\\n4. **Unlock divisions.** The 0.90 gate should be reconsidered — it's costing you the entire 0.1 division term, which is exactly the margin between 0.903 and the 0.92+ you're targeting. A division Jaccard of just 0.17 is worth +0.017, which closes the stated gap on its own.\\n\\nThe honest assessment: the governance scaffolding — frozen scorer parity, leakage-safe grouped splits, immutable manifests — is real engineering and worth keeping. But it has become the primary consumer of effort. Four days produced roughly a thousand report files, 88,000 lines of Python, and no movement on any score you own. The framework was built to prevent self-deception about progress, and it has succeeded at that narrow goal while preventing progress itself.\\n\\nSources: [[Kaggle competition](codex-text-link://https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/biohub-cell-tracking-during-development)](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/biohub-cell-tracking-during-development), [[image.sc announcement](codex-text-link://https://forum.image.sc/t/biohub-cell-tracking-during-development-kaggle-competition/121671)](https://forum.image.sc/t/biohub-cell-tracking-during-development-kaggle-competition/121671)\"],\"client-new-thread:6b6c3362-f82a-46cd-b963-f5c0383a6d19\":[\"can you trigger the LINE_CHANNEL_ACCESS_TOKEN rotation (ie just to test if the env var rotation will work on cron every 25 days), for both this repo and for the cleaning notifier then launch tests to check if both works\"],\"019f7b22-437b-7e73-89da-d5aa151605af\":[\"can u give the sha256 of the .env file for me to compare\",\"a5e52a6b31b7543cb38658428647ab770a0c5fd237f745f6b3b31926b6c60623 this is my sha256sum. so our .envs arentdifferent at all. even when i tested on localhost, it works, im not sure why doesnt it work on ur internal tests\"],\"client-new-thread:688484d7-174f-4587-9788-4620bcbf3be0\":[\"2s\\nRun npm ci\\n\\n> ezy@0.1.0 postinstall\\n> node packages/cli/dist/bin.js _install-codex --quiet --if-global\\n\\nnode:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1479\\n  throw err;\\n  ^\\n\\nError: Cannot find module '/Users/runner/work/eZY/eZY/packages/cli/dist/bin.js'\\n    at Module._resolveFilename (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1476:15)\\n    at wrapResolveFilename (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1049:27)\\n    at defaultResolveImplForCJSLoading (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1073:10)\\n    at resolveForCJSWithHooks (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1094:12)\\n    at Module._load (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:1262:25)\\n    at wrapModuleLoad (node:internal/modules/cjs/loader:255:19)\\n    at Module.executeUserEntryPoint [as runMain] (node:internal/modules/run_main:154:5)\\n    at node:internal/main/run_main_module:33:47 {\\n  code: 'MODULE_NOT_FOUND',\\n  requireStack: []\\n}\\n\\nNode.js v24.15.0\\nnpm error code 1\\nnpm error path /Users/runner/work/eZY/eZY\\nnpm error command failed\\nnpm error command sh -c node packages/cli/dist/bin.js _install-codex --quiet --if-global\\nnpm error A complete log of this run can be found in: /Users/runner/.npm/_logs/2026-07-18T00_44_56_150Z-debug-0.log\\nError: Process completed with exit code 1.\\n\\nthis is the issue w ci but shouldnt the git actions be rewritten to reflect that i dont hv a npm pkg but npm can be used to install git repos via the npm install git+... pattern?\"],\"019f7b3a-f6f3-7d23-87b5-3ec39dd20424\":[\"can you implement that?\"],\"019f7b15-b199-7781-a45e-d488fbab7002\":[\"create the codex prompt needed to fully implement this cronjob scheduler. I'll be pasting it into codex cli (railway cli is available)\"],\"019f7b07-a85f-73e0-a13d-aadaeb75566d\":[\"**The Cruise governance layer has detached from the work.** `.cruise/runs/sparse-hybrid/` hasn't been written since **Jul 17 20:47**. `state.yaml` still reads `updated_at: 2026-07-16`. `CLOSEOUT.md` was last written Jul 16 20:12. Meanwhile actual execution ran through **Jul 20 02:30**. So the last three days of work — the SSL prototype, temporal pretraining, the conflict-residual line — exist outside the goal framework entirely. Whatever the contract says about node state is now fiction.\\n\\nRanked by score per compute-hour, which is your stated criterion:\\n\\nNode-count calibration — roughly +0.10 on dense videos, 0 GPU-hours.\\nStop disabling the inherited divisions — up to +0.1 × division Jaccard, ~0 GPU-hours.\\nMaterialize more fold-0 fit videos, refit the conflict residual — I/O bound, not GPU bound.\\nRun fold0 holdout once — 0 GPU, ~4.3h CPU, makes everything else interpretable.\\nSSL — 1.75 GPU-hours minimum, mechanism-to-bottleneck fit unproven.\\nSSL is fourth or fifth, not first.\\n\\nBut SSL isn't worthless — it's misaimed\\nThere's a real distinction worth making, because it resolves your earlier pretraining question too. Your two sub-problems have opposite label economics:\\n\\nEdge association: labels are abundant (117 fit videos) and 90% unused. The right move is supervised scaling — same conflict-residual code, 50× the data. Consistency regularization under gamma/noise/flip augmentation buys invariance, not discrimination; if two candidate cells are genuinely similar in appearance and motion, making the model robust to brightness jitter doesn't separate them.\\n\\nDivisions: labels are genuinely scarce. Divisions are rare events and the Kaggle GT is sparse — which is exactly why the division head has never had anything to learn from and division_tp is 0 across 217 runs. Here transfer learning is well-motivated, and you already downloaded the corpus: Fluo-N3DH-CE.zip has 358 and 361 division parents across two sequences, Fluo-N3DH-SIM+ adds 37/40 with synthetic truth. Pretrain the division head there, fine-tune on Kaggle sparse labels.\\n\\nSo: transfer/pretraining for divisions, supervised scaling for edges, SSL for neither — yet.\\n\\nThe gate that would justify SSL\\nDon't adopt it as a direction; earn it with a cheap test. Scale the supervised conflict residual from 233 to ~10k pairs on materialized fold-0 videos. Then:\\n\\nIf supervised scaling keeps improving, you were label-starved by materialization and SSL is redundant.\\nIf it saturates well short of what you need, that's genuine evidence the representation is the limit — and then the 1.75 GPU-hours have a validated premise.\\n\\n**5. On pretraining specifically — you already own the right corpus and haven't used it.** You asked about pretraining for rapid convergence. The honest answer is that the Kaggle sparse GT gives almost no division signal, which is why the division head has never had anything to learn from. But `data/ctc/raw/Fluo-N3DH-CE.zip` (3.2 GB, already downloaded) contains **358 and 361 division parents** across two sequences of dense embryo lineages, and `Fluo-N3DH-SIM+` adds 37/40 with synthetic ground truth. That is a genuine division-pretraining corpus sitting unused on disk. Pretrain the division head there, fine-tune on Kaggle sparse labels. It's standard transfer learning, it's literature-validated, and it targets the 0.1 metric term that has been exactly zero in all 217 recorded runs.\\n\\n**6. Stop building divisions; stop disabling them.** `bh_graph.py:add_safe_divisions` already works — it's what gives the inherited V45 path its 429 divisions and its 0.903. Your local pipeline sets `enable_divisions=False` in `types.py:94`. Step 2 resolves the gate question empirically; if fold0 edge-only CV is already ≥0.90, the gate opens on its own evidence.\\n\\nThe through-line: four days went into building instruments and zero into taking the measurement they were built for. Fold0 is the measurement. Run it first, and most of the open questions answer themselves.\\n\\nour next goal should be determining what is the limiting factor, use the appropriate strategy given that limiting factor (ie if its scarce labels/data, then most of the corpus of 80gb must be used before that is determined), only then do we check if the code can complete 20% of the test corpus in 1h with a good >0.9 score before i make a kaggle submission, reducing likelihood of a timeout\",\"Yes, resume the goal and upload/start bh-fc00 and bh-fc01 as private fit-cache kernels. No competition submission.\",\"Error 1: the kernel failure (real, and the fix is genuine)\\nThe actual exception, from reports/g02/fitcache_remote/fc00_v1/bh9fail.json:\\n\\nFile \\\"/kaggle/working/bh9src/src/celltrack_alpha/bh_edge.py\\\", line 41, in <module>\\n    from .v45_head import V45AuxReadout\\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'celltrack_alpha.v45_head'\\nbh_edge.py:41 imports V45AuxReadout at module scope. The package shipped to Kaggle didn't contain v45_head.py, so the import blew up the moment celltrack_alpha.bh_edge was loaded. Both kernels died identically at 39.4 seconds, at the required_import_attestation stage, status: failed_closed — before any data reads.\\n\\nWhy the file was missing: scripts/pkgbh9.py builds the payload from a hand-written tuple called SOURCE_PATHS (line 86). That tuple listed seven celltrack_alpha modules and did not list v45_head.py. Nothing derives that list from actual imports — it's maintained by hand.\\n\\nThe timestamps tell the story: bh_edge.py was last modified Jul 19 14:42 (during the conflict-residual work, which is where the v45_head dependency came from). pkgbh9.py wasn't updated until Jul 20 12:34 — one minute after the kernels failed.\\n\\nThe fix is real. I decoded the base85-embedded bh9src.zip out of both fitc.py files:\\n\\nv1_failed/fc00/fitc.py → zip contains 18 files, no v45_head.py\\nfitcache/fc00/fitc.py → zip contains 19 files, v45_head.py present\\nSo the repaired package genuinely carries the missing module. That failure will not recur.\\n\\nTwo things worth noting. First, the failure design worked well — failing closed at an import probe in 39 seconds beats crashing two hours into a 22 GB read. Second, a residual gap: the REQ attestation tuple around line 513 lists modules to probe (celltrack_alpha.bh_model, .bh_graph, .bh_edge, …) and still doesn't name celltrack_alpha.v45_head — grep finds v45_head exactly once in the whole file, at line 93. That's not blocking, because bh_edge's probe catches it transitively (which is precisely what happened), but it's inconsistent.\\n\\nThe deeper issue is the class of bug, not this instance: any new intra-package import silently breaks remote packaging, because SOURCE_PATHS is manual. An AST import-walk at build time would make this structurally impossible. Right now you're one import away from repeating it.\\n\\nError 2: the loop you're actually watching (not a code error)\\nThis is what's burning your afternoon, and nothing is malfunctioning:\\n\\n12:21 — you authorized the upload; both kernels started\\n12:33 — both failed on v45_head\\n12:34 — agent diagnosed, patched, rebuilt, 15 tests pass\\n12:36 onward — agent tries to re-upload, gets blocked: re-uploading is an external code/artifact transfer requiring approval\\nYour 12:21 authorization was consumed by that specific upload. Permission is per-action, so a retry needs a fresh one. The agent is correctly blocked.\\n\\nMeanwhile the 10-minute heartbeat keeps firing — 12:43, 12:53, 13:03, 13:13 — and each wake does the only thing it can: check status, find ERROR, report \\\"awaiting approval,\\\" sleep. The heartbeat's own instructions say to \\\"retry only the same authorized private train-fit cache kernel if safe,\\\" but the retry needs a permission it doesn't hold.\\n\\nPolling cannot resolve a state that only your approval can unblock. The loop will run forever at zero information gain until you either grant the approval or kill the schedule.\\n\\nWhat to do\\nGrant the re-upload approval. The fix is verified — I confirmed 19 vs 18 files in the embedded zip, so you're not burning a run on an unvalidated guess. Scope is unchanged: fc00 = 52 videos / 22.4 GB, fc01 = 51 videos, T4x2, 8100s deadline, internet: false, private: true, competition_submission_performed: false.\\nFix or kill the heartbeat. As written it polls a frozen state. It should either wake on kernel completion, or simply stop and notify you once when it detects a blocked-on-approval condition — rather than re-reporting the same non-event every 10 minutes.\\nAfterwards, make SOURCE_PATHS derived rather than hand-maintained, so the next module you add to bh_edge.py doesn't cost you another failed Kaggle run and another approval round-trip.\\n\\n\\n\",\"approve, but do add comprehensive error logging to improve iterative debugging: example is whether Kaggle honored the shape and gave 1× T4 (shape works, count assumption wrong) or ignored it and gave a P100 again (shape string wrong). Those need different fixes, and the error message distinguishes them.\",\"last fragment written:  Jul 21 00:38\\nnow:                    Jul 21 11:17\\nfragments:              52 / 58\\nfinal report.json:      absent\\nprocesses burning CPU:  none (remaining matches have 0:00.00 CPU time)\\nThe run has produced nothing for 10 hours 39 minutes. It got 52 of 58 held-out videos — about 50 minutes short of finishing — and stopped. This is the measurement I said everything downstream depends on, and it has been silently dead since half past midnight.\\n\\nThe missing six are diagnostic:\\n\\ntrain:6bba_bb9f20c3   train:6bba_f1fde7e0\\ntrain:6bba_ebdf3b34   train:6bba_f4ae811c\\ntrain:6bba_f17befbc   train:6bba_fbc898dc\\nAll six are 6bba — the dense scenario, the one where v6diag.json recorded 2,550 predicted nodes against 553 for a 44b6 video. They're also the last six in sort order. So this is either a tail-end stall or, more likely, resource exhaustion on the densest videos. A silent stop with no traceback and no CPU burn points at memory rather than an exception.\\n\\nThe monitoring gap\\nThe 10-minute heartbeat was watching bh-fc00 and bh-fc01 — kernels that had already succeeded. Nothing at all was watching the local fold-0 job. So the poll dutifully reported \\\"both kernels running normally, no action needed\\\" while the thing that actually mattered was dying.\\n\\nThat's the second time the monitor has been aimed at a settled state. The fix the agent added (\\\"pause instead of looping if another approval boundary appears\\\") addresses the deadlock case but not this one: normal-operation polling of a healthy remote job, with zero coverage of the local critical path.\\n\\nDon't restart from scratch\\nThe 52 fragments are intact — 2,149,194 rows, zero empty or header-only files. That's a real 52-video holdout, far beyond anything measured in this project before (previous best: 12 videos scored).\\n\\nTwo things to do, in this order:\\n\\nScore the 52 now. You get your first genuine multi-video number today, including the first-ever division Jaccard. Label it 52/58, not the full holdout — but don't let a 90%-complete result sit unscored while you re-run.\\nRun the missing six separately, with memory instrumentation. If they OOM individually, that's a real finding about dense-video inference that also bears on your submission runtime — those are the videos that will be slowest and heaviest on Kaggle too.\\nAlso worth noting from the timings: 52 videos across 17:24→00:38 is ~501 s/video on CPU, roughly double the 266 s I was working from. Against the measured 73 s/video on T4×2, that's a ~6.9× CPU:GPU ratio — which keeps the submission runtime projection comfortable, but means local CPU evaluation is an overnight job every time. Worth moving holdout evals to GPU.\\n\\nOn the diagnostics\\nThe implementation is exactly right. The classification tree does precisely the disambiguation I wanted:\\n\\nunexpected_accelerator_family → shape string wrong (the P100 case)\\nhonored_t4_wrong_device_count → shape honored, count assumption wrong\\nno_cuda_devices / exact_t4x2\\nAnd including torch_compiled_arch_list was a good call — that would have surfaced the sm_60-vs-sm_70+ incompatibility directly rather than leaving it buried in a PyTorch warning.\\n\\nOne gap: it's in pkgbh9.py, which builds the fit-cache kernel. The submission kernel is built by pkgbh.py → bh45.py, and that path has zero occurrences of the new classification logic. Your next external action is the submission, and it currently ships without any of this. Port it before you build that package.\\n\\n\\nso keep running the remaining 10% of the unrun videos while scoring the 52 videos' results simultaneously\"],\"client-new-thread:2c9c4b28-b358-43d7-93c0-d841587a8bc1\":[\"Refine `validation/run_snucrop_benchmark.py` into a rigorous, reproducible benchmark for Python cellSNP versus cellsnp-fast.\\n\\nThe benchmark currently has several methodological problems:\\n\\n1. Python cellSNP defaults to one process while cellsnp-fast defaults to up to eight.\\n2. Live memory is obtained by summing process-tree RSS from `ps`.\\n3. Missing PIDs or incomplete `ps` snapshots are silently converted into zero RSS.\\n4. Shared pages can be counted repeatedly when worker RSS values are summed.\\n5. Both runs share one absolute wall-time x-axis, visually compressing the much shorter run.\\n6. The final post-exit sample can appear as zero and create a misleading vertical drop.\\n7. A short C++ run may be undersampled relative to the Python run.\\n\\nImplement the following changes without weakening the existing output-equivalence checks.\\n\\n## 1. Benchmark configurations\\n\\nSupport three explicit benchmark suites.\\n\\n### A. Single-process implementation comparison\\n\\nRun:\\n\\n- Python cellSNP with `-p 1`\\n- cellsnp-fast with `--nprocs 1`\\n\\nTreat this as the primary result and label it:\\n\\n`single-process implementation comparison`\\n\\nDo not describe this as a pure language-only comparison because the algorithms and data structures differ.\\n\\n### B. Equal-worker scaling comparison\\n\\nAdd a CLI option:\\n\\n`--worker-counts 1,2,4,8`\\n\\nFor each worker count N, run:\\n\\n- Python cellSNP with `-p N`\\n- cellsnp-fast with `--nprocs N`\\n\\nRecord for every implementation and N:\\n\\n- wall time\\n- user CPU time\\n- system CPU time\\n- total CPU time\\n- peak memory\\n- median memory\\n- p95 memory\\n- output size\\n- process count\\n- speedup relative to that implementation at N=1\\n- parallel efficiency, defined as:\\n  `speedup_from_1_worker / N`\\n\\n### C. Practical configuration comparison\\n\\nOptionally run each program with a separately configured worker count:\\n\\n- `--python-practical-nprocs`\\n- `--fast-practical-nprocs`\\n\\nKeep this result separate from the single-process comparison.\\n\\n## 2. Robust Linux memory measurement\\n\\nPrefer cgroup v2 accounting when available.\\n\\nFor each benchmark run:\\n\\n1. Create a dedicated transient cgroup under a configurable parent.\\n2. Place the launched benchmark process into that cgroup immediately.\\n3. Poll:\\n   - `memory.current`\\n   - `memory.peak`, when available\\n   - `memory.events`\\n4. Record the cgroup memory timeline.\\n5. Clean up the cgroup after all descendants exit.\\n\\nAdd options:\\n\\n- `--memory-backend auto`\\n- `--memory-backend cgroup`\\n- `--memory-backend pss`\\n- `--memory-backend rss`\\n\\nIn `auto` mode:\\n\\n1. Use cgroup v2 when writable and supported.\\n2. Otherwise use process-tree PSS.\\n3. Fall back to RSS only when neither is available.\\n\\n## 3. PSS fallback\\n\\nImplement a process-tree PSS collector using:\\n\\n`/proc/<pid>/smaps_rollup`\\n\\nFor every currently living process in the benchmark process tree, parse:\\n\\n- `Pss`\\n- `Rss`\\n- `Private_Clean`\\n- `Private_Dirty`\\n- `Shared_Clean`\\n- `Shared_Dirty`\\n\\nSum PSS across the process tree.\\n\\nUse PSS as the authoritative fallback metric because it allocates shared pages proportionally rather than counting them fully once per process.\\n\\nHandle races safely:\\n\\n- A PID may exit between process-tree enumeration and file reading.\\n- Catch `FileNotFoundError`, `ProcessLookupError`, and `PermissionError`.\\n- Skip only the vanished PID.\\n- Do not convert a failed entire sample into zero.\\n\\n## 4. Eliminate false zero-memory samples\\n\\nThe current monitor effectively does:\\n\\n`rss_by_pid.get(pid, 0)`\\n\\nThis creates misleading zero samples when the root process is missing from an incomplete `ps` snapshot.\\n\\nReplace this behaviour with explicit sample validity.\\n\\nEach sample must contain:\\n\\n- timestamp\\n- memory value\\n- backend\\n- root_pid_present\\n- process_count\\n- valid\\n- invalid_reason, when invalid\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n1. If the root PID should still be alive but cannot be found, mark the sample invalid.\\n2. Do not append zero as a substitute.\\n3. Do not connect invalid samples in the graph.\\n4. Preserve the last valid sample separately.\\n5. After process exit, do not append a synthetic live-memory sample.\\n6. Store final peak memory from cgroup `memory.peak`, `wait4`, or the maximum valid sampled value.\\n7. If a final zero is needed for lifecycle semantics, store it as an explicit event:\\n   `process_exited`\\n   Do not include it as a normal memory sample.\\n8. Reject isolated zero values between positive samples as invalid unless the selected backend explicitly confirms zero memory while the process is alive, which should normally be impossible.\\n\\nAdd diagnostics counting:\\n\\n- valid samples\\n- invalid samples\\n- root-missing samples\\n- process-enumeration failures\\n- per-PID read races\\n- backend failures\\n\\n## 5. Sampling\\n\\nAdd:\\n\\n- `--sample-interval`, default `0.5`\\n- `--python-sample-interval`\\n- `--fast-sample-interval`\\n\\nUse a monotonic clock.\\n\\nThe monitor should:\\n\\n1. Take an initial valid sample immediately after process launch.\\n2. Poll at the requested interval.\\n3. Avoid cumulative drift by scheduling against absolute monotonic deadlines.\\n4. Take no synthetic live sample after process reaping.\\n5. Preserve timestamps at full floating-point precision in JSON and CSV.\\n\\n## 6. Process-tree tracking\\n\\nDo not rely exclusively on a single `ps` text snapshot.\\n\\nOn Linux, prefer `/proc/<pid>/task/<pid>/children` recursively, with a safe fallback to `/proc/*/stat` PPID enumeration.\\n\\nAccount for:\\n\\n- processes that fork after sampling starts\\n- workers that exit between samples\\n- PID reuse\\n- the root process exiting before every child is reaped\\n\\nWhere possible, also record process start time from `/proc/<pid>/stat` so PID reuse is not mistaken for process continuity.\\n\\n## 7. Plotting\\n\\nGenerate these plots.\\n\\n### A. Separate absolute-time panels or files\\n\\nCreate one absolute wall-time graph per run or per implementation and worker count.\\n\\nDo not place a 500-second run and a 25,000-second run on one absolute x-axis without an additional normalized view.\\n\\n### B. Normalized progress graph\\n\\nCreate a graph using:\\n\\n`elapsed_time / total_wall_time`\\n\\non an x-axis from 0 to 100%.\\n\\nThis graph may overlay Python and C++.\\n\\nLabel it clearly as normalized elapsed time, not equivalent computational progress.\\n\\n### C. Raw and smoothed memory\\n\\nShow:\\n\\n- faint raw valid samples\\n- rolling maximum over a configurable window\\n- optional rolling median\\n\\nAdd:\\n\\n`--memory-smoothing-window-seconds`\\n\\nDo not interpolate through invalid samples.\\n\\n### D. Scaling plots\\n\\nGenerate:\\n\\n- wall time versus worker count\\n- speedup versus worker count\\n- parallel efficiency versus worker count\\n- peak memory versus worker count\\n- CPU time versus worker count\\n\\n## 8. Statistical repetition\\n\\nAdd:\\n\\n- `--repetitions`, default `3`\\n- `--warmup-runs`, default `0`\\n\\nFor each configuration, report:\\n\\n- median wall time\\n- minimum wall time\\n- maximum wall time\\n- median peak memory\\n- median total CPU time\\n- coefficient of variation\\n\\nPreserve every individual run in the JSON output.\\n\\nRandomize or alternate execution order to reduce systematic cache and thermal bias. Record the actual order.\\n\\n## 9. Cache-policy metadata\\n\\nDo not drop the Linux filesystem cache automatically unless explicitly requested and permitted.\\n\\nAdd:\\n\\n- `--cache-policy warm`\\n- `--cache-policy alternating`\\n- `--cache-policy explicit-drop`\\n\\nDefault to `alternating`.\\n\\nRecord:\\n\\n- whether input files were likely cached\\n- execution order\\n- whether cache dropping was attempted\\n- whether it succeeded\\n- storage device metadata when available\\n\\n## 10. Resource controls\\n\\nAllow optional CPU and memory isolation:\\n\\n- `--cpu-list`\\n- `--memory-limit`\\n- `--cgroup-parent`\\n\\nWhen a CPU list is supplied, pin the benchmark root and descendants consistently.\\n\\nRecord:\\n\\n- logical CPU count\\n- assigned CPU IDs\\n- total system RAM\\n- operating system\\n- kernel version\\n- compiler version\\n- Python version\\n- pysam version\\n- HTSlib version\\n- Git commit hashes for both implementations\\n- full commands\\n- environment variables relevant to threading\\n\\nPrevent hidden library parallelism where applicable by setting and recording:\\n\\n- `OMP_NUM_THREADS`\\n- `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS`\\n- `MKL_NUM_THREADS`\\n- `NUMEXPR_NUM_THREADS`\\n\\nDefault these to 1 unless explicitly overridden.\\n\\n## 11. Output files\\n\\nProduce:\\n\\n- `benchmark_report.json`\\n- `benchmark_report.md`\\n- `memory_samples.csv`\\n- `memory_samples_<implementation>_<workers>_<repeat>.csv`\\n- separate absolute-time SVG or PNG graphs\\n- normalized-memory graph\\n- scaling graphs\\n- benchmark log\\n\\nThe CSV sample schema should include:\\n\\n- run_id\\n- implementation\\n- worker_count\\n- repetition\\n- time_seconds\\n- normalized_time\\n- memory_bytes\\n- metric_type\\n- backend\\n- process_count\\n- root_pid_present\\n- valid\\n- invalid_reason\\n\\n## 12. Report interpretation\\n\\nThe Markdown report must separate:\\n\\n1. Single-process implementation speedup.\\n2. Equal-worker throughput comparison.\\n3. Parallel scaling efficiency.\\n4. Practical configured throughput.\\n5. Memory comparison.\\n6. Output-equivalence status.\\n7. Measurement limitations.\\n\\nExplicitly warn that:\\n\\n- aggregate RSS can double-count shared pages\\n- PSS is an estimate\\n- cgroup memory includes all processes assigned to the group\\n- normalized wall-time percentage does not represent equal algorithmic progress\\n- single-process speedup reflects the complete implementation, not only the programming language\\n\\n## 13. Tests\\n\\nAdd focused automated tests for:\\n\\n1. Root PID temporarily missing from enumeration.\\n2. Worker exiting during `/proc` inspection.\\n3. Invalid samples not appearing as zero.\\n4. Invalid samples causing gaps rather than connected graph segments.\\n5. Final process exit not adding a zero live-memory point.\\n6. PSS parsing.\\n7. cgroup memory parsing.\\n8. backend fallback order.\\n9. process-tree recursion.\\n10. PID reuse detection where implemented.\\n11. normalized timestamps.\\n12. equal-worker command construction.\\n13. repetition aggregation.\\n14. parallel-efficiency calculation.\\n\\nUse small fixture processes that allocate memory, fork children, release memory, and exit.\\n\\n## 14. Backward compatibility\\n\\nPreserve:\\n\\n- the existing SNUCROP input preparation\\n- output-equivalence checks\\n- SHA-256 comparisons\\n- existing authoritative output files\\n- existing benchmark report fields where reasonable\\n\\nNew fields may be additive.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\nThe implementation is complete only when:\\n\\n1. Python and C++ can be benchmarked at 1, 2, 4, and 8 equal workers.\\n2. A dedicated 1-versus-1 headline result is generated.\\n3. Missing process samples never appear as zero RSS or PSS.\[stdout]
\n4. Graphs contain gaps for invalid samples.\\n5. No ordinary final sample drops vertically to zero after process exit.\\n6. cgroup v2 is used when available.\\n7. PSS is the functional fallback.\\n8. each run reports its memory backend.\\n9. repeated runs are aggregated statistically.\\n10. output equivalence remains unchanged.\\n11. tests pass.\\n12. documentation explains how to reproduce the benchmark exactly.\\n\\nBefore modifying code, inspect the current benchmark, process-launch logic, memory monitor, graph generation, and report schema. Then provide:\\n\\n1. a concise implementation plan\\n2. the code changes\\n3. tests\\n4. commands to run the single-process and scaling suites\\n5. a summary of methodological limitations that remain\"],\"019f7e32-f91c-7223-a7e7-e28297987d9c\":[\"can i use that directly in the rhel shell\"],\"client-new-thread:2af2c7f4-d845-4b6d-bd35-81978e5c0b7f\":[\"i will be establishing an npm pkg, cruise-cli v0.1.0, and require an index js file. Does one already exist? If not create it, structure the files in a manner compatible with npm \"],\"019f808b-14e6-7263-a432-4d295f51f2d1\":[\"what type should i set my npm pkg as and are the keywords: codex plugin meta-prompting agentic automation good- tbh they arent very useful too as idt npm is good for seo?\",\"how to replace the keywords if i've already submitted it; or do i manually edit before manually pushing it on npm\",\"agent@Mus-MacBook-Pro cruise % npm whoami\\nnpm view cruise-cli name version maintainers --json\\nmuchups\\nnpm error code E404\\nnpm error 404 Unpublished on 2021-11-25T02:07:23.362Z\\nnpm error 404\\nnpm error 404  The requested resource 'cruise-cli' could not be found or you do not have permission to access it.\\nnpm error 404\\nnpm error 404 Note that you can also install from a\\nnpm error 404 tarball, folder, http url, or git url.\\n{\\n  \\\"error\\\": {\\n    \\\"code\\\": \\\"E404\\\",\\n    \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Unpublished on 2021-11-25T02:07:23.362Z\\\",\\