t claude-compat- this is supposed to be highly optimised/compact so for parts like TUI it shouldnt change much, but the underlying plumbing should be fundamentally claude-optimised just like the current cruise is codex-optimised- the idea is that you have these 2 toggles, and each activates the respective parameter sets (the entire codebase is to be generalised sufficiently to accomodate both llm, such as the escape character to indicate a command is / for claude while it is $ for codex, all the way to how codex has an exposed /goal and /plan mode endpoint for my plugin to activate/deactivate/pause, but im not sure about claude's analogous funtions\"],\"client-new-thread:6fa03cab-e208-420b-bd94-6ae4730e858f\":[\"evaluate how cruise:goal performs relative to codex's goal, and cruise:refine vs codex's /plan or claude's plan mode and how the other 3 functions compare with their closest plugin analogues \"],\"client-new-thread:3ab9cc17-f8ea-4c0f-8aa5-bd5c5684f361\":[\"Read reports/g02/source_softmax_edge_f0_fit117_holdout_prefix52_v1/scores.json\\nbefore doing anything else.\\n\\nSTOP GATING THIS RUN ON >= 0.90. It is arithmetically unreachable and waiting\\nto \\\"check if total > 0.9\\\" is dead time. Proof, from that file's own samples:\\n\\n  W52 (sum of edge_tp+edge_fp+edge_fn over the 52 scored videos) = 34,146\\n  weighted adjusted edge Jaccard = 0.855066 (reproduces the reported summary)\\n  the 6 unscored videos are all 6bba; 6bba mean w = 904, so W6 ~ 5,427 (13.7%)\\n\\n  Ceiling if all 6 score a perfect adj edge of 1.00 : 0.87494\\n  Ceiling if all 6 score 1.07 (max node-bonus)     : 0.88454\\n\\nBoth are below 0.90. Reaching 0.90 would require W6 >= 15,343 (at cap 1.0) or\\n>= 9,025 (at 1.07). We have ~5,427. Verify this arithmetic yourself from the\\nsamples array, then record it as a finding and close the >=0.90 gate question\\nfor this run on evidence rather than leaving it pending.\\n\\nCHANGE OF BEHAVIOUR\\n1. Finish the six remaining 6bba videos and score the full 58 — for the\\n   complete citable number and to confirm the division behaviour is not\\n   scope-specific. Do NOT treat the result as a pass/fail against 0.90.\\n2. Report the 58-video score plainly, including division_tp/fp/fn.\\n3. Do not create a Kaggle submission without fresh explicit approval.\\n\\nPARALLEL INVESTIGATION (start now, do not wait for the six)\\n\\nA. DIVISION BUG — highest priority, worth up to 0.1 of score.\\n   Enabling divisions produced division_tp=0, division_fp=133, division_fn=37\\n   across 52 videos. Note GT divisions are rare: TP+FN = 37 total, ~0.7/video.\\n   Critically, our geometry constants ALREADY MATCH the scored V45 reference:\\n   division_candidate_child_um=4.66 vs safe_division_parent_um=4.66, and\\n   division_sister_um=8.5 vs safe_division_sister_um=8.5. V45's sibling V103\\n   emits 429 divisions. So this is NOT parameter drift — it is structural.\\n   Read external/kaggle-cell-tracking-competition/src/tracking_cellmot/\\n   division_metrics.py and check src/celltrack_alpha/bh_graph.py\\n   add_safe_divisions against what the metric actually rewards. Specifically\\n   test: (i) is the fork attached at the correct timepoint given the +/-1\\n   tolerance; (ii) do the matched predicted nodes lie in ONE connected\\n   component as the metric requires; (iii) is the parent/sister distance test\\n   applied to the right node pair; (iv) does prune_graph or postprocess_graph\\n   remove the fork after it is added. Write a focused failing test that\\n   reproduces a known GT division from the holdout before changing any logic.\\n   Given 133 FP / 0 TP is net-negative (each false fork also adds an edge FP),\\n   default divisions back OFF until a test demonstrates a true positive.\\n\\nB. PER-VIDEO NODE THRESHOLD. Mean over-prediction is +1.7% but the tail is\\n   severe: 6bba_705ec2c9 +0.707, 6bba_07e24132 +0.672, 44b6_996155de +0.501\\n   (edge Jaccard 0.984 collapsing to adjusted 0.935 on the node penalty alone).\\n   Evaluate an adaptive per-video or per-scenario threshold against the frozen\\n   global 0.985, using only fit-side data to set it. Note the adjustment\\n   multiplier is UNCAPPED above 1.0, so slight under-prediction is rewarded.\\n\\nC. ERROR BUDGET. From the 52 samples compute, weighted by\\n   w_i = edge_tp+edge_fp+edge_fn, how much adjusted edge Jaccard is lost to\\n   edge FP, to edge FN, and to the node-count penalty separately. State how\\n   many edge events must be corrected to reach 0.90 and 0.92. This tells us\\n   which lever is actually large enough to matter — note the conflict residual\\n   moved the score only +0.00185 despite a 7.85pp gain in conflict accuracy,\\n   so small-share levers are not worth further investment.\\n\\nCONSTRAINTS: read-only on Kaggle. No submission, no paid compute, no public\\nartifacts. Preserve the frozen split and scorer. Report findings with file,\\nline, and numbers; distinguish verified from inferred.\"],\"019f82ec-a8de-7960-b5fc-8924329d3b31\":[\"i just cloned the repo into the cluster. tell me step by step what to do - do i make? first then run the python3 validation/run_snucrop_benchmark.py \\\\\\n  --worker-counts 1,2,4,8 \\\\\\n  --python-practical-nprocs 4 \\\\\\n  --fast-practical-nprocs 8 \\\\\\n  --repetitions 3 \\\\\\n  --work-dir validation/benchmarks/snucrop_scaling_practical\"],\"019f82cb-cda3-7192-ab6b-e5157300e3b7\":[\"Stop work on 6bba_f4ae811c and on the 58-video completion. The ceiling is\\nalready established to within ±0.005 and the 58th fragment cannot change any\\ndecision. Record the run as complete-at-57 with that caveat and move on.\\n\\nKeep every finding and artifact produced so far. F1-F4 are sound; the division\\ngeometry measurement in F4 is the single most useful result of the week and it\\ncorrectly invalidates an externally suggested \\\"allow re-parenting\\\" fix.\\n\\nFIRST, commit. The repository has one commit (c3b9b703) and a week of work is\\nuntracked. Create a branch and commit the current state before any further\\nedits, so there is a rollback point. Do not push.\\n\\nTRAJECTORY CHANGE - read this carefully.\\n\\nDivisions are not an independent problem. Place F4 next to the fixed-node\\noracle in reports/g02/t04_v45f4_fold0_fixed_node_oracle/: with the SAME node\\nset and edges rewired against ground truth, divisions score tp=37 fp=0\\n(divJ 0.881) and adjusted edge reaches 0.9750. Meanwhile 35/37 real divisions\\ncurrently fail solely because matched nodes land in disconnected components.\\n\\nTherefore: association fragmentation is the single root cause of BOTH the edge\\ndeficit AND the zero division term. The 22.9% geometry envelope you measured is\\na symptom - in a fragmented graph the required bridge must span a long distance\\nbecause the intermediate structure is absent. Do not widen the geometry gates;\\ndo not build a division head; do not tune division constants. All of these are\\ndownstream.\\n\\nKeep divisions default-OFF as you decided. That is correct and stays.\\n\\nNEW PRIORITY - characterise the fragmentation, read-only, no approval needed:\\n1. Across the 57 scored videos, quantify where prediction components break\\n   relative to GT tracks. Pooled prediction component size is 23.4 nodes vs\\n   31.9 for GT tracks - locate the breaks in time and space.\\n2. Test the hypothesis that breaks concentrate in crowded scenes. The strongest\\n   per-video correlate of low edge Jaccard is peak_component_matrix_dim\\n   (Pearson -0.702), far ahead of total_node_ratio (-0.083). Confirm or refute\\n   on the full 57.\\n3. For each break, classify the cause: candidate absent from the KD-tree gate,\\n   candidate present but out-ranked at a conflict, gate radius too tight, or\\n   solver constraint (degree/one-parent) forcing the drop. Report counts.\\n4. State how many of the 1,810 edge FN and 2,660 edge FP are attributable to\\n   each class.\\n\\nDo NOT start new model training, do not touch Kaggle, do not create a\\nsubmission. Report findings with file, line, and numbers, and clearly separate\\nverified from inferred - your discipline on this so far has been good.\"],\"client-new-thread:ea60b214-447b-40e4-abb9-5e68dfc5f049\":[\"/goal # Orchestrator — execute the full Cruise v0.3 DAG in one run\\n\\nYou are executing a six-task implementation plan for the `cruise` repository\\n(the current working directory). The six task specifications already exist\\nas files in `prompts/`; **they are the authoritative specs — this document\\nonly sequences them and sets ground rules.** Read each one in full\\nimmediately before starting that task, and follow it exactly, including its\\nacceptance criteria and its design-philosophy section.\\n\\n## Execution order\\n\\nRun the tasks **serially, on the current branch**, in this order:\\n\\n```text\\n1. prompts/A-hygiene.md            (delete retired v0.1 control plane)\\n2. prompts/B-flight-plan.md        (.cruise/FLIGHT.md + flight-plan.mjs)\\n3. prompts/C-codex-supervisor.md   (stall supervisor in the Codex goal loop)\\n4. prompts/D-claude-hooks.md       (Stop-hook supervisor for Claude Code)\\n5. prompts/E-build-queue.md        (.cruise/QUEUE.md + `cruise queue run`)\\n6. prompts/F-evals-and-docs.md     (paired evals + docs reconciliation)\\n```\\n\\nBecause you are one session on one branch, the specs' inter-task rules\\ntranslate as follows:\\n\\n- **\\\"Files you must not touch\\\" lists mean \\\"not during this task.\\\"** They\\n  were written to keep parallel sessions mergeable; for you they are phase\\n  scope fences. Do Task C's work in Task C, not opportunistically during\\n  Task B.\\n- **\\\"Expected merge conflict\\\" notes do not apply.** Additions to\\n  `package.json` (`files`, `test`) simply accumulate across tasks.\\n- **The C∥D decoupling rule still stands as written:** D's hook script\\n  re-implements its own small fingerprint helper rather than importing\\n  `supervisor.mjs`, and marks it with a consolidation TODO. Do not\\n  \\\"improve\\\" this by sharing code — the decoupling is deliberate (the hook\\n  must run as a standalone process from the packaged plugin).\\n- Where a spec says another task \\\"owns\\\" a file you later reach (e.g. C owns\\n  `LIMIT_POLICY.md`, F owns the README rewrite), that ownership arrives\\n  when you reach that task.\\n\\nIf `prompts/` is untracked, commit it first (\\\"add v0.3 implementation\\nspecs\\\") so history records what was executed.\\n\\n## Gates between tasks\\n\\nAfter each task, before starting the next:\\n\\n1. Run `npm test` — must be green, including that task's new suites.\\n2. Run `node scripts/cruise.mjs --help` — must succeed.\\n3. Run `claude plugin validate .` if the `claude` CLI is available;\\n   otherwise note its absence once and continue.\\n4. Confirm the task's own acceptance-criteria checklist item by item.\\n   An unmet criterion is a blocker for that task, not a note for later.\\n5. Commit in the reviewable slices the spec asks for. Never carry\\n   uncommitted work across a task boundary.\\n\\nIf a gate cannot be satisfied after genuine remediation attempts, stop the\\nrun there and report: which task, which criterion, what you tried, and what\\nevidence or decision is needed. Do not proceed to a later task on top of a\\nfailed gate, and do not weaken a criterion to pass it.\\n\\n## Ground rules for the whole run\\n\\n- **The design philosophy in the specs is non-negotiable.** Artifacts\\n  (`FLIGHT.md`, `QUEUE.md`) are advisory compiled intent, never authority;\\n  supervision is evidence-based, user-tunable, and fail-safe (ladders end\\n  in pause-with-report / allow-with-report). If an implementation choice\\n  seems to require an approval object, ledger, digest, archive, or\\n  run-control authority, the choice is wrong — find the advisory-shaped\\n  alternative or stop and report the tension.\\n- Do not redesign the specs. If two specs genuinely conflict at a seam\\n  (e.g. an API detail B pinned that C needs shaped differently), resolve\\n  it with the smallest change, note the deviation explicitly in that\\n  task's commit message and your final report, and keep the pinned public\\n  contracts (`FLIGHT.md` schema, `flight-plan.mjs` API, supervisor verdict\\n  levels, `QUEUE.md` schema) stable — later tasks and documentation\\n  depend on them verbatim.\\n- No new runtime dependencies anywhere in the run.\\n- Keep code style matched to the existing live modules\\n  (`simple-session.mjs`, `origin-note.mjs`): plain ESM, no frameworks,\\n  `node --test` for tests.\\n\\n## Task F specifics\\n\\nTask F's paired evaluations require live hosts. Run `cruise doctor` for\\nboth providers first; execute every trial the environment supports, build\\nthe remainder as runnable fixtures, and record honestly what stayed gated.\\n**Never fabricate, extrapolate, or summarize a trial that did not run.**\\nA partial-but-honest F is a successful F; an embellished one is a failed\\nrun.\\n\\n## Final report\\n\\nEnd with: tasks completed with commit ranges; total lines deleted (A) and\\nadded; every deviation from a spec with its rationale; gate results per\\ntask; eval verdict or gated-status summary from F; and the single next\\naction you would recommend.\"],\"019f6f64-07f4-7f13-bce5-623df143ed39\":[\"how should i run map_read now and give me a list of all the expected inputs and their preset directories - and which files theyre found in\",\"the paths are incorrect- it is absolute: $HOME/scratch/data/references/... (also note about file name spaces- flag them if any other paths contain that- the dot bracket for ribo for mouse is now fully underscored)\"]},\"has-opened-quick-chat-v1\":true,\"thread-descriptions-v1\":{\"019f711f-f7eb-71d0-b51f-462050028374\":\"cell_tracking G-02 V6 checkpoint fit/CV predictions and scoring bridge\",\"019f7120-062c-71e3-ad0b-2999c22f9918\":\"Read-only Cruise audit in cell_tracking: run_alpha_routed_zip.py, 203-record registry, smoke logs\",\"019f711f-e97a-7862-90